# Load tests

```bash
# Against your local stack
K6_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080 \
K6_EMAIL=you@example.com K6_PASSWORD=... \
  k6 run load/smoke.js
```

## Rules

- **Staging, never production**, and never against infrastructure you do not own.
  A load test against someone else's service is a denial-of-service attack.
{{#if multiTenant}}- **Authenticate.** Every real query runs inside a tenant context and RLS adds a
{{else}}- **Authenticate.** Every real query runs behind a guard, and the guarded path is
{{/if}}
{{#if multiTenant}}  filter to each one. A test that skips auth measures a code path that does not
{{else}}  the one under load. A test that skips auth measures a code path that does not
{{/if}}
  exist in production.
- **Thresholds, not graphs.** `options.thresholds` makes k6 exit non-zero on a
  regression, so this can gate a deploy. A load test whose only output is a chart
  gets looked at once.
- **Percentiles, not means.** p95 and p99 are what users feel. A mean of 100ms
  hides a p99 of four seconds.

## Where it belongs in CI

Nightly against staging, not on every pull request — it takes minutes and needs a
warm environment to mean anything. Wire it as a scheduled job and alert on the
threshold failure.
